---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Layout from "../layouts/layout.astro";
import ClosingInvitation from "../components/closing-invitation.astro";
import OrnamentRule from "../components/ornament-rule.astro";
import NumeralLozenge from "../components/numeral-lozenge.astro";

import ColorFusionImg from "../assets/images/color-fusion.png";
import EmblemImg from "../assets/images/s-emblem.png";
import StainedGlassImg from "../assets/images/stained-glass.jpg";
import CelticKnotImg from "../assets/images/gallery/3-Celt6x6-300_600.jpg";
import FinishedHeartImg from "../assets/images/gallery/Finished-heartsm_d600.jpg";
import MiningProofImg from "../assets/images/gallery/Coal-Mining-Machine1_d600.png";
import RubySlipperImg from "../assets/images/gallery/Ruby-slipper_d600.png";
import { patentNotice } from "../data/site";

const chapters = [
  {
    numeral: "I",
    title: "Colored glass history",
    paragraphs: [
      "For hundreds of years, colored glass has been produced and used in many ways. A little-known fact is that colored glass never fades: it holds its original color indefinitely.",
      "In Europe, some of the oldest cathedrals still have their original art glass. Superb examples survive from the early 12th century, such as King David in Augsburg Cathedral in Germany, and the 13th-century windows of Notre-Dame de Chartres in France. Art glass made over 800 years ago is still as vibrant and beautiful as the day it was created.",
    ],
    image: StainedGlassImg,
    imageAlt: "A medieval cathedral window of blue and red stained glass",
    isArched: true,
  },
  {
    numeral: "II",
    title: "Shaping the glass",
    paragraphs: [
      "Today we have numerous modern tools, such as diamond-crusted saw blades and grinders. The computer-controlled water-jet, with an ultra-thin cutting radius, is essential for cutting glass.",
      "Cutting a one-inch glass letter or number is no longer a challenge, because the machine’s tolerances are measured in thousandths of an inch.",
    ],
    image: CelticKnotImg,
    imageAlt: "A finely cut Celtic knot glass inlay set in granite",
    isArched: true,
  },
  {
    numeral: "III",
    title: "Fusing and annealing",
    paragraphs: [
      "Computer-controlled kilns are responsible for a high-quality outcome. Barometric pressure, air temperature and humidity still play a role, but good record keeping and testing ensure the highest quality in glass finishing.",
      "To fuse, fire-polish and anneal an inlay, the glass is heated multiple times and spends well over 30 hours in the kiln. Our techniques ensure a perfect finish and a hardness up to 200 times that of regular plate glass.",
    ],
    image: FinishedHeartImg,
    imageAlt: "A finished, fire-polished glass heart held in two hands",
    isArched: true,
  },
  {
    numeral: "IV",
    title: "Stencils",
    paragraphs: [
      "Before a stencil can be cut, a graphic artist draws the artwork and designs the layout in Adobe Illustrator, the gold standard among graphic art and layout programs. Most plotting programs work like a CAD program, which engineers and architects favor, with hints of Illustrator, which artists and the printing industry use.",
      "The stencil we create is custom matched to the finished glass art. When an artisan sandblasts the stencil onto the granite, the fit of the glass inlay into the stone is guaranteed.",
    ],
    image: MiningProofImg,
    imageAlt:
      "A client’s photograph of a mining machine, our designed proof, and the finished inlay on a headstone",
    isArched: false,
  },
  {
    numeral: "V",
    title: "Installation",
    paragraphs: [
      "The glass is bonded to the granite with an industrial, epoxy-like adhesive made specially to adhere glass to stone. A tensile strength of over 2,500 pounds ensures a bond that will never be challenged.",
      "The only enemy of the adhesive is ultraviolet light from the sun. So every design has a small outline filled with cement-based grout, which seals the gap permanently and keeps the adhesive out of the sunlight. The grout adheres permanently to glass and granite, and we color it with UV-fast pigments, adding another layer of sophistication to the design.",
    ],
    image: RubySlipperImg,
    imageAlt: "A ruby slipper and rainbow glass inlay, outlined in grout, on a granite marker",
    isArched: true,
  },
];
---

<Layout title="Our Product">
  <header class="crimson-wash relative isolate overflow-hidden border-b border-border">
    <div class="shell grid items-center gap-10 py-14 sm:py-20 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_auto]">
      <div class="grid max-w-3xl gap-5">
        <Image
          src={ColorFusionImg}
          alt="ColorFusion"
          width={320}
          height={74}
          class="wordmark-on-dark -ml-2 h-auto w-56 sm:w-64"
        />
        <h1 class="text-4xl leading-[1.08] sm:text-5xl lg:text-6xl">
          Permanent colorfast glass inlays
        </h1>
        <OrnamentRule class="max-w-xs" />
        <p class="prose-measure text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          Colored glass holds its original color indefinitely. These are the
          methods and materials we use to bring it to granite.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Image
        src={EmblemImg}
        alt=""
        widths={[240, 480]}
        sizes="220px"
        loading="eager"
        class="emblem-glow illuminate hidden w-52 lg:block"
      />
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="py-16 sm:py-24">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow text-gilt">Methods and materials</p>
      <ol class="mt-10 grid gap-20 sm:gap-28">
        {
          chapters.map((chapter, index) => (
            <li class="grid items-center gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2 lg:gap-20">
              <div class:list={["grid gap-5", index % 2 === 1 && "lg:order-2"]}>
                <div class="flex items-center gap-4">
                  <NumeralLozenge numeral={chapter.numeral} />
                  <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl">{chapter.title}</h2>
                </div>
                <div class="prose-measure grid gap-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
                  {chapter.paragraphs.map((paragraph) => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}
                </div>
              </div>
              <figure class="mx-auto w-full max-w-md lg:max-w-none">
                <div
                  class:list={[
                    "bg-came",
                    chapter.isArched ? "arched-frame aspect-[5/4]" : "came-frame rounded-lg",
                  ]}
                >
                  <Image
                    src={chapter.image}
                    alt={chapter.imageAlt}
                    widths={[400, 800]}
                    sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 45vw, 90vw"
                    class:list={["w-full", chapter.isArched ? "h-full object-cover" : "h-auto"]}
                  />
                </div>
              </figure>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>

      <div class="mx-auto mt-24 grid max-w-3xl justify-items-center gap-5 rounded-lg border border-gilt/40 bg-card px-6 py-12 text-center sm:px-12">
        <NumeralLozenge numeral="VI" isOnCard />
        <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl">Patent</h2>
        <OrnamentRule class="w-40" />
        <p class="max-w-xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          Our process is special and unique enough that we applied for national
          and international patents. Our first patent has been granted.
        </p>
        <p class="eyebrow text-foreground">{patentNotice}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <ClosingInvitation />
</Layout>
